@startuml i2c-register-read
' Reading one register over I2C1, which is the shape every ADXL345 access
' takes. Grounded in Src/i2c.c (i2c1_write_then_read, begin_transfer,
' wait_for, force_stop) and in STM32L476.svd for the register and bit names.

title One register read, and the three ways it can end badly

skinparam shadowing false

participant "caller" as app
participant "i2c1_write_then_read" as drv
participant "I2C1\n(peripheral)" as per
participant "device\n(ADXL345 at 0x53)" as dev

app -> drv : address, register number, one byte out
activate drv

drv -> per : CR2 = address, NBYTES 1, write, no AUTOEND, START
note right of drv
  AUTOEND is deliberately OFF here.
  The bus must stay held so the read
  can open with a repeated start.
end note
per -> dev : start, address, write

alt device answers
  per --> drv : TXIS
  drv -> per : TXDR = register number
  per -> dev : register number
  per --> drv : TC, the write half is done

  drv -> per : CR2 = address, NBYTES n, read, AUTOEND, START
  per -> dev : repeated start, address, read
  dev --> per : the bytes
  loop for each byte
    per --> drv : RXNE
    drv -> per : read RXDR
  end
  per --> drv : STOPF
  drv -> per : ICR = STOPCF
  drv --> app : I2C_OK
else device not plugged in, or holding SCL
  note over drv
    No flag ever arrives. core_millis is
    free running, so the 5 ms budget
    expires even with the bus stuck.
  end note
  drv -> per : CR2 |= STOP
  drv --> app : I2C_TIMEOUT
else device says no
  per --> drv : NACKF
  drv -> per : ICR = NACKCF
  drv -> per : CR2 |= STOP
  drv --> app : I2C_NACK
end

deactivate drv

note across
  Every exit puts a stop on the bus and clears the flag behind it.
  A BERR left standing would be read as the NEXT transfer's error.
end note

@enduml
